.TH CHMAP_CONSTRUCT 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chmap_construct \- type-inferred hash map construction
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

chmap_construct(hm_name, key_type, val_type);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Type-inferred macro that creates a hash map with the default settings.
It determines the key and value types automatically. It also creates
type marker variables. These variables tell the other type-dispatching
macros the declared key and value types at each call site.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name   Name of the hash map variable to create
key_type  Type of keys
val_type  Type of values
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Stops the program if the allocation fails
Creates hidden type marker variables that the other type-dispatching
macros read. chmap_redeclare(3) creates them again in another scope
Uses CCOL_DEFAULT_INITIAL_BUCKET_ARRAY_SIZE (16) buckets
Uses the default memory management and the default hashing
.fi
.SH "FLOAT AND DOUBLE KEY EQUALITY"
.nf
For a float or double key_type, key equality is bitwise equality.
It is not IEEE 754 == equality. There is one exception: the map
canonicalizes -0.0 and 0.0 to the same key. This matches
-0.0 == 0.0 in C.
Every other bit pattern obeys bitwise equality, NaN included. The
map therefore always finds a NaN key again by the exact bit pattern
that the caller inserted it with. (In C, NaN == NaN is always
false.) Two different NaN payloads are two different keys.
.fi
.SH "LONG DOUBLE KEY EQUALITY"
.nf
For a long double key_type, key equality uses the numeric value
(native ==). It does not use bitwise equality. The keys -0.0L and
0.0L are the same key. Two keys that hold the same value stay the
same key. This is also true when their platform-defined padding
bits are different. Every NaN long double collapses into one key.
This is different from float and double, where each different NaN
payload is a different key.
The map does not use this value-based handling when the caller
gives a custom hashing function at creation time. See
chmap_create_full(3).
.fi
.SH "STRUCT KEY EQUALITY"
.nf
For a key type that the map does not know, such as a struct, key
equality compares every byte of the key, padding included, and the
default hash reads every byte too. The macros set the padding of
their own copy of a key to zero with GCC 11 or later. With any other
compiler that padding is unspecified, and a padded struct key then
needs a custom hashing function and a custom key equality function,
given through chmap_construct_full(3). See chmap_create_full(3).
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
chmap_construct(map, int, char*);
int key = 42;
chmap_insert(map, key, "hello");
chmap_destroy(map);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_construct_full (3),
.BR chmap_create (3)
